Connect orbital and transit evidence to planetary systems (@physics-astronomy-planets: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Build-a-bounded-stellar-model-from-gravity-pressure-and-energy.lingua#r_00F8BE3ACB1A7ECBAFAB896747") { r_0061F9B72C184476321D5F90D0
Use [Derive orbital shape timing and the limits of parameter inference](Derive-orbital-shape-timing-and-the-limits-of-parameter-inference.lingua#r_003B0A54AD3132E93CD761C01A) for the relative two-body orbit: P²=4π²a³/[G(m₁+m₂)]. Here a is the physical semimajor axis of the relative orbit, not one star's barycentric radius or its projected angular separation at one epoch. A visual orbit needs projection geometry and distance. For an isolated binary, m₁r₁+m₂r₂=0 about its center of mass, so the barycentric semimajor axes satisfy a₁/a₂=m₂/m₁ and a=a₁+a₂.

For a circular orbit, the line-of-sight velocity amplitudes are K₁=(2π/P)a₁ sin i and K₂=(2π/P)a₂ sin i, with i=0 face-on. Their ratio gives m₂/m₁ when both are measured, but their scale leaves an inclination ambiguity. Adding the amplitudes and using the period relation gives (m₁+m₂)sin³i=P(K₁+K₂)³/(2πG). A face-on system has zero projected amplitudes even with substantial orbital speed. Eccentric cases add the appropriate √(1−e²) factor and phase model; do not use the circular expression unmodified.

For a dark small planet fully overlapping a uniformly bright stellar disk, ignoring limb effects and contaminating light, the fractional transit depth is δ=(R_p/R_*)². With additional constant light F_c relative to stellar flux F_*, it becomes δ=(R_p/R_*)²/(1+F_c/F_*). A radius ratio 1/10 without dilution and 1/5 with contamination ratio 3 both give depth 1/100. Transit depth alone gives neither planet mass nor a unique physical radius. Grazing geometry, limb darkening, stellar variability and blended eclipses require further evidence.

Planetary science connects these remote constraints to material properties, gravity, rotation, internal heating, atmospheres and surface history. For a uniformly reradiating spherical body with Bond albedo A_B and emissivity ε, absorbed power πR²(1−A_B)F_* balances 4πR²εσT_eq⁴, so T_eq⁴=(1−A_B)F_*/(4εσ). This is an ideal energy-balance temperature, not automatically a surface temperature or habitability test. Atmospheres, internal heat and incomplete heat redistribution alter it; geological and chemical interpretation uses Gaia and chemistry where developed.

**Work:** derive the circular projected mass relation, then use the shared orbit evaluator to recover center-of-mass position and momentum for changed masses. Construct the two transit rivals and name evidence that distinguishes them. Compare a planet receiving four times the stellar flux under otherwise identical assumptions: its equilibrium temperature grows by √2, not by four. Larger chapters 7–14 and 21 of [Astronomy second edition by OpenStax](../sources/Astronomy-second-edition-by-OpenStax.lingua#r_00466D09500F8EEF3B1876A23F) remain branch reading rather than prerequisites to every common lesson.

**Continue:** [Build a bounded stellar model from gravity pressure and energy](Build-a-bounded-stellar-model-from-gravity-pressure-and-energy.lingua#r_00F8BE3ACB1A7ECBAFAB896747).

